issues/123 -- `str()`/`print()` do not fall back to a class's `__repr__`: pyc prints "<object>" where CPython prints the __repr__ text, with zero warnings and exit 0. In CPython object.__str__ calls self.__repr__(); pyc implements the two as unrelated methods. Found when ifa/124's splitter fix let `go` run for the first time and its board still printed as "<object>". The check file holds CPython's output and this flips to PASS when the delegation lands. The D class guards the reverse direction: a class defining __str__ only must keep the default repr(), so this must not be fixed with an alias.
